<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mobutu Sese Seko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mobutu Sese Seko was the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, which he renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>, from 1965 to 1997. An army officer, he seized power in a coup in November 1965 and ruled as an authoritarian president backed by Western powers during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, promoting a policy of 'authenticity' while his regime became a byword for corruption. He was overthrown in 1997 by Laurent-Désiré Kabila's rebels, backed by Rwanda and Uganda, and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko.jpg" alt="Mobutu Sese Seko" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Hall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mobutu_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mobutu, born Joseph-Désiré Mobutu on 14 October 1930 at Lisala in the Belgian Congo, belonged to the Ngbandi ethnic group. His mother, Marie Madeleine Yemo, was a hotel maid, and her husband, Albéric Gbemani, was a cook for a Belgian judge. Gbemani died when Mobutu was eight, and an uncle and a grandfather raised him.</p><p>He found discipline and a father figure in the colonial army, in Sergeant Louis Bobozo, and read European newspapers and books whenever he could, among them the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>. In 1956 he left the army and became a journalist in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a>, writing for the daily L'Avenir. He later joined the Congolese National Movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> and became his personal aide. Several accounts say he was also an informer for Belgian intelligence and for the Americans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the mutiny of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/force-publique">Force Publique</a> on 5 July 1960 Mobutu helped negotiate the release of officers and their families. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a>, as Chief of Staff of the army, he deposed Lumumba's government with American and Belgian support. He installed a government that arranged for Lumumba's execution in 1961, and he continued to lead the armed forces.</p><p>In March 1965 parliament twice refused to confirm the prime minister-designate Évariste Kimba, and the government was in near-paralysis. On 24 November 1965 Mobutu took power in a bloodless coup. He assumed almost absolute powers under a state of exception, and in March 1966 his government permanently suspended parliament. In 1967 he made the Popular Movement of the Revolution the only legal party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Mobutu pursued a campaign of cultural change called authenticité. From 1 June 1966 he renamed cities, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> became Kinshasa and Stanleyville Kisangani, and in October 1971 he renamed the country <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>. People were ordered to take African names, Western dress was banned, and men wore the Mao-style abacost. He also put rivals to death: Kimba and three ministers were hanged on 30 May 1966 before an audience of 50,000. His rule involved a cult of personality, widespread corruption and the expropriation of foreign-held businesses under Zairianisation.</p><p>His opposition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> won support from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. In May 1990 he gave up the party's monopoly, and troops killed at least 290 students at Lubumbashi on 12 May, after which Western countries ended non-humanitarian aid. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rwandan-genocide">Rwandan genocide</a> of 1994, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> refugees and fighters based in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> destabilised the region. In May 1997 rebels under Laurent-Désiré Kabila overran the country. Mobutu left on 23 May, lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, and died of prostate cancer on 7 September 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mobutu Sese Seko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…overthrew the elected government of Salvador Allende, and Argentina in 1976, and repeatedly in Nigeria, Ghana and many other African states; independence leaders banned rival parties; and personal rulers such as <b>Mobutu Sese Seko</b> in Zaire (1965–1997) and Ferdinand Marcos in the Philippines (1965–1986) held power for decades.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Party membership is usually a minority of the population, though <b>Mobutu Sese Seko</b>’s Zaire enrolled every citizen in its ruling movement.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lisala, in what is now <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">the Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=2.14,21.51&amp;year=1973" aria-label="Lisala on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5147 2084 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mobutu Sese Seko"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5597 2354h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5597 2354h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=2.14,21.51&amp;year=1973">What was here in 1973? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
